  "summary": "A wood surface aesthetic featuring irregular, swirled grain patterns from deformed tree growth.",
  "description": "Characterized by intricate, swirling patterns and eyes resulting from tree deformities, used for decorative veneers and furniture. Historically valued for its rarity and unique appearance, burl wood has been prized by woodworkers and artisans across cultures for centuries.",
  "culturalContext": "Burl figuring — the swirled grain of tree-growth deformities — is wood's most expensive chaos: the dormant buds, birdseye maple \"eyes\", walnut and redwood burl crotch-figuring prized by carvers (shokkotsu Japanese root sculpture), rifle-stock makers, dashboards and boutique guitar tops. Because burls defy milling stability, each panel is a negotiation; burl veneer became mid-century furniture's luxury signifier (Danish modern tea carts) and stays the \"exotic wood\" default in luxury interiors.",
